    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's what I implemented for the spec \u2014 *\"a new owner's membershipLevel cannot exceed one above the current maximum membershipLevel among their household members; with no existing household member no cap applies.\"*\n\n## Changes\n\n**1. The cap itself** \u2014 new `MembershipCap` helper (`rest/function/owner/MembershipCap.java`). Since `membershipLevel` is derived (not stored on `Owner`), the cap is recomputed wherever the final per-owner level is produced. `cappedLevelFor(owner, ownLevel, repo)`:\n- Returns `ownLevel` unchanged when the owner has no `householdId` **or** no *other* household member exists (the \"no cap\" case).\n- Otherwise returns `min(ownLevel, maxOtherMemberLevel + 1)`, scoring each other member with the same household size for a deterministic ceiling. The owner is excluded from its own household by id, so it works whether or not the new owner is already persisted.\n\nApplied it at the three surfaces that finalize the level: `RespondWithOwner` (GET), `RespondWithOwnerCreated` (POST 201), and `AuditOwnerCreated` (audit log).\n\n**2. Enabling multiple household members** \u2014 the new test needs two owners in one household (same last name + postcode, different telephone/email) to coexist, but `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` was blocking *any* second owner on `householdId` alone. The already-built-but-unused `OwnerIdentityKey` documents the intended rule (duplicate only when the *whole* key \u2014 telephone|email|household \u2014 matches), and `AssignPossibleDuplicate` has a documented \"same household, different telephone\" branch that was dead under the old block. I switched `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` to compare full identity keys, which lets distinct household members coexist while still rejecting true re-submissions, and updated the stale docs on it and `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException`.\n\nThe audit output confirms the behavior end-to-end: the two same-household owners were both created, with the second's level landing at 2 (\u2264 the first's level 1 + 1).\n\nOne thing worth flagging: change #2 loosens duplicate detection (same-household owners with different phone numbers are now allowed rather than 409'd). That was necessary for the test to reach the cap logic at all, and it's consistent with the codebase's own `OwnerIdentityKey`/`AssignPossibleDuplicate` design \u2014 but it's a behavioral change beyond the literal one-line spec, so call it out if that duplicate-blocking was intended to stay strict.",
